Section 5 - Policy Statement
(4)
All members of the La Trobe University community are responsible for the preservation of confidentiality integrity and availability of University information. They must:
comply with all legislative, regulatory and contractual requirements;
follow incident management processes;
abide by People & Culture and user access security procedures;
follow communication and connectivity management processes;
follow asset management, physical security, and allocation processes;
follow business continuity management practices;
use approved acquisition, development and maintenance practices;
follow physical and environmental security procedures;
follow access control procedures;
undertake security education, training and awareness; and
make themselves aware of the consequences of information security policy violations and adhere to the
Data Governance
and
Records Management
policies.
(5)
Under the
Use of Computer Facilities Statute 2009
the CIO may issue determinations from time to time with prospective effect to determine authorised usage. The CIO is empowered to determine the levels of compliance to the practices contained in this policy and procedure.

Responsibilities
(7)
The Security team are responsible for:
developing and maintaining the Information Security policy and procedures;
investigating security incidents and taking corrective actions;
communicating security risks and awareness to employees; and
reviewing and updating security controls based on changing threats.
(8)
Third-Party Vendors are responsible for:
adhering to the university security standards when accessing sensitive data;
implementing robust security practices in their own operations; and
providing necessary security documentation upon request.
(9)
System Owners are responsible for:
ensuring data classification and protection for their assigned systems;
implementing appropriate security controls based on data sensitivity; and
reporting data breaches or potential incidents to the security team.
(10)
IT Administrators are responsible for:
monitoring for potential security breaches and incidents;
patching systems promptly to address vulnerabilities;
managing user access controls and privileges;
conducting regular security assessments and audits;
the protection and secure disposal of physical information assets; and
clear identification and inventory of physical information assets.
(11)
All staff are responsible for:
reporting suspicious activity to IT security immediately;
maintaining strong passwords and not sharing credentials;
using university-approved devices for work-related activities;
completing mandatory security awareness training regularly; and
properly handling sensitive data according to university guidelines.
